<p>Last December, I gave a talk at <a href="https://emacsconf.org/2025/talks/zettelkasten/">EmacsConf 2025</a> called “Zettelkasten for Regular Emacs Hackers”. <a href="/posts/2025/12/join-all-virtual-emacsconf-december-6--7-zettelkasten-talk/">I announced it back then</a>, but never pointed to the recording. Here it is.</p>

<figure class="post-figure youtube"><div class="video-container"><iframe src="https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/wE8vCWyr1Eo" title="YouTube video" loading="lazy" frameborder="0" allowfullscreen=""></iframe></div><figcaption>Zettelkasten for Regular Emacs Hackers, EmacsConf 2025 (23 minutes)</figcaption></figure>

<p>The pitch: writing improves the quality and depth of thought, and a Zettelkasten is an environment for thinking through writing, not merely a place to store notes. Nobody can hand you a finished thinking environment the way they can hand you an email client. You have to build your own, and Emacs is the most moldable place to do it.</p>

<p>In 23 minutes, I walk through:</p>

<ul>
  <li>the three essential mechanics: <strong>write</strong> (with effort), <strong>connect</strong> (with links), and <strong>correct</strong> (improve notes as you learn more);</li>
  <li>the habits that make it scale over decades: design for use, create structure, start in the Zettelkasten, and start with a link;</li>
  <li>why “garbage in, garbage out” applies to notes, too;</li>
  <li>a live demo with a minimal <code>init.el</code> and <a href="https://protesilaos.com/emacs/denote">Denote</a> to create, link, rename, and backlink notes. <a href="#try-it-yourself">Try it yourself</a> with the configuration and <a href="#the-notes-from-the-demo">the notes from the demo</a> below.</li>
</ul>

